{"skill":{"slug":"yahoo-finance-cli","displayName":"Yahoo Finance CLI"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1769720161795,"changelog":"Initial release.","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":4173,"sha256":"4e686411019bf4978325eefe09716755359d29cd678c85378cadde4349c06ae6","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2136,"sha256":"1d52b4ea05e86dc6502087619293a7e344144c0d18670ffeec473768591f7967","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789023744751,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"2983d6755f58dcbcabf2949e40461a8543719b832798026ca61e72598e2d2078","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/2983d6755f58dcbcabf2949e40461a8543719b832798026ca61e72598e2d2078","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: yahoo-finance-cli\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe OpenClaw skill 'yahoo-finance-cli' is classified as benign. Its purpose is to fetch financial data from Yahoo Finance, which is clearly aligned with its stated goal. Installation uses standard package managers (Homebrew for `jq`, npm for `yahoo-finance2`) and creates a symbolic link, all common practices. The only potentially risky command, `rm -f ~/.yf2-cookies.json`, is explicitly for troubleshooting a cookie file created by the skill itself, not for arbitrary file deletion or system compromise. There is no evidence of data exfiltration, malicious execution, persistence mechanisms, or prompt injection attempts to subvert the agent's behavior for harmful purposes.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160330635},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":48,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","issueCount":4,"checkedAt":1789023744751},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This Yahoo Finance skill is mostly purpose-aligned, but its install instructions can persistently modify a system-wide command path and use an unpinned npm package.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Node.js, jq, Yahoo Finance network access, and a local cookie file are proportionate to the purpose, but writing a global executable link is broader than needed for a CLI helper skill.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The metadata install command creates /usr/local/bin/yf with ln -sf from a dynamically resolved npm global binary, and the user-facing install block also recommends a sudo symlink. The npm dependency is unpinned, so install-time behavior can change after review.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The activation wording is broad for financial data requests, but the actual runtime examples stay within Yahoo Finance market-data queries. The cookie deletion troubleshooting step is limited to the named yahoo-finance2 cookie file.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The system-wide yf symlink persists across sessions and may overwrite an existing command path. No backdoor, service, scheduler, exfiltration, or hidden destructive behavior was found.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill's stated purpose is to fetch stock quotes, earnings, financial summaries, search results, historical chart data, and trending symbols using the Yahoo Finance CLI with jq filtering; the documented usage matches that purpose.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"ok"}],"guidance":"Review the install steps before using this skill. Prefer a user-local or project-local install, pin and audit yahoo-finance2, avoid sudo and forced /usr/local/bin symlinks unless you have verified the target binary and existing yf command, and understand that yahoo-finance2 may store cookies in ~/.yf2-cookies.json.","findings":"[TM1] expected: The rm -f ~/.yf2-cookies.json command is disclosed troubleshooting for a specific Yahoo Finance cookie file; it is not broad deletion or hidden destructive behavior.\n[SQP-1] expected: The trigger language is broad but still centered on stock market and Yahoo Finance data, so it is a scoping note rather than a standalone security concern.\n[PE2] unexpected: Creating a CLI alias is setup-related, but requiring sudo to write into /usr/local/bin is overbroad and lacks validation or a safer user-local alternative.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: The artifact includes system-changing install commands and file deletion guidance without clear warnings; this contributes to the Review verdict when combined with the global link behavior.\n[T08] unexpected: Installing yahoo-finance2 is expected, but the artifact does not pin a reviewed version or integrity hash, leaving dependency behavior mutable after review.\n[T07] unexpected: The metadata command uses ln -sf to replace /usr/local/bin/yf with a dynamically resolved npm binary, which can persistently redirect future yf invocations and is not necessary for the stated purpose.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789023744751}}}}}